## Follow-up: delete the Sphinx build output, don't just ignore it

Fair challenge on the previous commit: gitignoring 46MB of stale generated HTML
left it sitting in the working tree. Established what consumes it before removing
it:

| Consumer | Verdict |
|---|---|
| The app at runtime | No. The Help Center serves `documentation/*.md` directly; nothing in `src/` references Sphinx. |
| CI | Build-smoke only — `ci.yml` runs `make html` to check Sphinx still builds, with no artifact upload and no deploy. |
| GitHub Pages | No. `docs/` is a separate hand-written marketing site (10 tracked files); its README explains that Pages can only publish `/` or `/docs`, which is why product docs live in `/documentation`. No Pages workflow, no CNAME, no `.nojekyll`. |
| Releases | No reference in `release.yml` or `nightly.yml`. |

So it is purely local build output. Deleted from disk, after confirming the
previous commit still has all 330 files should anyone want them back.

Verified it regenerates: `cd documentation/sphinx && make html` → "build
succeeded, 19 warnings". The fresh build is **327 files, not 330** — the three
missing ones are exactly `SyncedTableManager.html`, `LakebaseFlatStore.html` and
`LakebaseBase.html`, pages for modules P5 deleted. The committed output had been
drifting from the source tree, which is the argument against committing build
output in the first place. The renamed `guides/postgres-graphdb.html` and
`api/app.core.graphdb.postgres.html` both render.

`make clean` now sweeps `documentation/sphinx/_build`, so it cannot silently
accumulate again — the gitignore stops it being committed, the clean target stops
it being hoarded.

## Round 2: deletions and consolidation

Nothing in `src/` is dead — a full scan for modules no file references anywhere
returned **0**. The findings were all misplacement rather than rot.

**Postgres tuning constants moved out of the vendor package.**
`back/core/databricks/lakebase/constants.py` held SQLSTATE classification, retry
backoff, pool sizing and `application_name` labels, all server-agnostic. Three of
its four consumers (the shared pool, the graph pool, the registry store) are
server-agnostic too; only `LakebaseAuth` is Lakebase-specific. So the generic
Postgres layer was importing out of a vendor package, because Lakebase was once the
only Postgres target. Moved to `back/core/postgres/constants.py` beside
`PostgresAuth` and `PostgresConnectionPool`, comments de-vendored — the pool
lifetime is now justified against the shortest credential lifetime *any* supported
auth mode issues, which is the actual constraint. No compatibility shim: a
re-export would be the scatter being removed. The `lakebase` package is left with
`LakebaseAuth` + `grants` + `__init__`, all genuinely Lakebase, so it got more
coherent rather than just smaller.

**Two operational guides moved out of a PR-artefact folder, and four dead paths
fixed.** `documentation/pr47-neo4j-demo/` is its own README's "proof artefacts for
PR #47". Two files in it were not artefacts but durable guidance that **production
error messages point at**, so they moved to `documentation/`. Every one of those
four source references was broken: they said `docs/pr47-neo4j-demo/…`, and `docs/`
is the GitHub Pages marketing site — the docs live in `documentation/`. A user
hitting a Neo4j auth failure was told to read a path that does not exist. Same
class of bug as the `.gitignore` that never matched. Both guides are now registered
in the Help Center, since only registered slugs are served and
`neo4j-requirements.md` links to them.

**Checked and deliberately kept:** `requirements.txt` (CI's `pip-audit` reads it —
deleting it breaks the Security Scan; header rewritten to state the real purpose
instead of the deleted Apps build); `NOTICE.txt` and `SECURITY.md` (zero inbound
refs but convention files read directly by GitHub and license compliance);
`scripts/migrations/*`; `licenses/`; the 17 convention files, whose scatter is
deliberate because Cursor reads `.mdc` natively with per-glob scoping.

**Recommended but not done:** the remaining 13MB of `pr47-neo4j-demo/` — 7.6MB of
screenshots, a 5.4MB PDF and an HTML deck, unreferenced now that the operational
content is out. Unlike the Sphinx `_build`, this is authored and unregenerable
(screenshots of one run on a specific workspace), so deleting another contributor's
verification evidence is not a call to make unilaterally.

