## "app service principal" in user-facing messages

The phrase appears at ~30 sites. They split three ways, and only one group was
wrong:

**Fixed — 8 user-facing messages** telling an operator to grant a privilege to
"the app service principal". There is no Databricks App any more; OntoBricks
authenticates as a service principal via `DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID` **or** as the
signed-in user (which is what enables per-user Unity Catalog enforcement on
interactive queries). Each now names the identity:

| Site | Was | Now |
|---|---|---|
| `uc/UnityCatalog.py` ×2 | "grant USE SCHEMA / READ VOLUME to the app service principal" | "…to the identity OntoBricks connects as (DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID, or the signed-in user)" |
| `registry/RegistryService.py` | "check the app service principal's CREATE VOLUME privilege" | "check the CREATE VOLUME privilege of the identity OntoBricks connects as" |
| `domain/Domain.py` ×2 | "Grant SELECT … to the app service principal" / "The service principal can list tables" | named identity / "OntoBricks can list tables" |
| `shared/fastapi/health.py` ×3 | check label, remediation (`TO \`<app-sp>\``), and description | named identity; the GRANT template now says `` `<principal>` `` |

**Kept — genuinely about Databricks Apps service principals.** Verified before
touching: `grants.resolve_app_service_principals` really does call
`/api/2.0/apps/{name}` for a `service_principal_client_id`, so it and its callers
(`store.py`) and `PermissionService`'s ACL docstring mean exactly what they say.
Renaming them would have been wrong.

One of those is user-reachable, though, so it now says which world it belongs to:
"Could not resolve any **Databricks App** service principal to grant … This path
applies only to a Databricks Apps deployment; on a container deployment, grant
Postgres privileges directly (see `documentation/postgres-graphdb.md` §5)."

**Left alone — ~15 internal docstrings** saying "the app's service principal" as
shorthand for the connecting identity. Imprecise but not user-facing; changing them
is churn without a reader benefit.

